2026-07-21T03:27:30.9995726Z     The ``pytest`` framework makes it easy to write small tests, yet
2026-07-21T03:27:30.9996250Z     scales to support complex functional testing for applications and libraries.
2026-07-21T03:27:30.9996649Z     
2026-07-21T03:27:30.9996851Z     An example of a simple test:
2026-07-21T03:27:30.9997107Z     
2026-07-21T03:27:30.9997313Z     .. code-block:: python
2026-07-21T03:27:30.9997551Z     
2026-07-21T03:27:30.9997756Z         # content of test_sample.py
2026-07-21T03:27:30.9998028Z         def inc(x):
2026-07-21T03:27:30.9998251Z             return x + 1
2026-07-21T03:27:30.9998475Z     
2026-07-21T03:27:30.9998653Z     
2026-07-21T03:27:30.9998851Z         def test_answer():
2026-07-21T03:27:30.9999094Z             assert inc(3) == 5
2026-07-21T03:27:30.9999333Z     
2026-07-21T03:27:30.9999521Z     
2026-07-21T03:27:30.9999713Z     To execute it::
2026-07-21T03:27:30.9999921Z     
2026-07-21T03:27:31.0000104Z         $ pytest
2026-07-21T03:27:31.0000397Z         ============================= test session starts =============================
2026-07-21T03:27:31.0000761Z         collected 1 items
2026-07-21T03:27:31.0000987Z     
2026-07-21T03:27:31.0001194Z         test_sample.py F
2026-07-21T03:27:31.0001527Z     
2026-07-21T03:27:31.0001786Z         ================================== FAILURES ===================================
2026-07-21T03:27:31.0002329Z         _________________________________ test_answer _________________________________
2026-07-21T03:27:31.0002695Z     
2026-07-21T03:27:31.0002895Z             def test_answer():
2026-07-21T03:27:31.0003149Z         >       assert inc(3) == 5
2026-07-21T03:27:31.0003416Z         E       assert 4 == 5
2026-07-21T03:27:31.0003674Z         E        +  where 4 = inc(3)
2026-07-21T03:27:31.0003927Z     
2026-07-21T03:27:31.0004140Z         test_sample.py:5: AssertionError
2026-07-21T03:27:31.0004494Z         ========================== 1 failed in 0.04 seconds ===========================
2026-07-21T03:27:31.0012119Z     
2026-07-21T03:27:31.0012325Z     
2026-07-21T03:27:31.0013217Z     Thanks to ``pytest``'s detailed assertion introspection, you can simply use plain ``assert`` statements. See `getting-started <https://docs.pytest.org/en/stable/getting-started.html#our-first-test-run>`_ for more examples.
2026-07-21T03:27:31.0014174Z     
2026-07-21T03:27:31.0014364Z     
2026-07-21T03:27:31.0014563Z     Features
2026-07-21T03:27:31.0014769Z     --------
2026-07-21T03:27:31.0014970Z     
2026-07-21T03:27:31.0015558Z     - Detailed info on failing `assert statements <https://docs.pytest.org/en/stable/how-to/assert.html>`_ (no need to remember ``self.assert*`` names)
2026-07-21T03:27:31.0016202Z     
2026-07-21T03:27:31.0016404Z     - `Auto-discovery
2026-07-21T03:27:31.0016857Z       <https://docs.pytest.org/en/stable/explanation/goodpractices.html#python-test-discovery>`_
2026-07-21T03:27:31.0017356Z       of test modules and functions
2026-07-21T03:27:31.0017618Z     
2026-07-21T03:27:31.0017997Z     - `Modular fixtures <https://docs.pytest.org/en/stable/explanation/fixtures.html>`_ for
2026-07-21T03:27:31.0018535Z       managing small or parametrized long-lived test resources
2026-07-21T03:27:31.0018872Z     
2026-07-21T03:27:31.0019243Z     - Can run `unittest <https://docs.pytest.org/en/stable/how-to/unittest.html>`_ (or trial)
2026-07-21T03:27:31.0019705Z       test suites out of the box
2026-07-21T03:27:31.0019952Z     
2026-07-21T03:27:31.0020150Z     - Python 3.10+ or PyPy3
2026-07-21T03:27:31.0020395Z     
2026-07-21T03:27:31.0020968Z     - Rich plugin architecture, with over 1300+ `external plugins <https://docs.pytest.org/en/latest/reference/plugin_list.html>`_ and thriving community
